WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Ammaryar invites you to watch this documentary film.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:38.000
[Aftermath of the missile strikes on Shajareh Tayyebeh Primary School in Minab]

3
00:00:40.000 --> 00:01:28.000
[On February 28, 2026 (9 Esfand 1404), following US and Israeli strikes on Shajareh Tayyebeh Primary School
in Minab, 168 innocent people — mostly schoolchildren, teachers, and parents — were martyred.]

4
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:36.000
"Today our students in Minab were martyred in blood and dust
at Shajareh Tayyebeh Primary School."

5
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:23.000
[Rescuers and parents amidst the rubble]
"Come this way! Hurry over here!"

6
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:54.000
"O Lord of the Age! Look at what they have done to these children..."

7
00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:13.500
[News Anchor] The United Nations Children's Fund expressed deep alarm
over the killing of children in the US and Israeli attacks on Iran.

8
00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:26.800
UNICEF reported that 180 Iranian children were killed in these strikes, more than 160 of whom
were students at Shajareh Tayyebeh Primary School in Minab.

9
00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:30.820
[Narrator] Soheil Chamelipour was only eight years old.

10
00:04:30.820 --> 00:04:40.820
He is one of the 165 martyrs of the Israeli and American missile strike
on Shajareh Tayyebeh Primary School in Minab.

11
00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:45.520
[Soheil Chamelipour, archival clip] "Here, here, here, and here —
we should leave these parts white."

12
00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:50.920
[Narrator] His mother flips through Soheil's Persian writing workbook with longing.

13
00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:58.420
[Soheil's Mother] The workbook asked: "Write in four lines what you want to be when you grow up."
His answer was: "I will build missiles to stop Israel."

14
00:04:58.420 --> 00:05:02.520
[Narrator] Soheil had an important reason for writing that sentence.

15
00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:10.460
[Soheil's Mother] When I saw Israel's crimes killing children, I cried.
Soheil asked me: "Mom, why are you crying?"

16
00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:18.320
I told him: "The Israelis are killing children." Soheil said to me:
"Mom, one day I will build missiles to stop Israel."

17
00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:45.080
[Alireza Zarei, Director] Western Minab, Saturday, Esfand 9 [February 28, 2026] —
one of the most severe, atrocious, and bitter crimes in human history.

18
00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:58.080
In my view, in the view of history, and across all media that have reported from here,
we truly cannot find any parallel to this atrocity in human history.

19
00:05:58.080 --> 00:06:15.200
Nothing is beyond those who caused such a tragedy. Here stands Shajareh Tayyebeh
Girls' and Boys' Primary School in Minab — also named Rahpouyan-e Shohada-ye Khalij-e Fars —

20
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:27.360
where over 180 children — girls and boys — along with teachers and parents
were martyred. Here is their story.

21
00:06:46.440 --> 00:07:02.280
[Archival footage — students rehearsing a school play before the strike]
[Teacher] "Are you ready, children? Memorize your lines and speak out loud with confidence!"

22
00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:22.280
[Students rehearsing in the schoolyard]
"I went to the front! I faced the enemy! I called out: Coward! Tremble!"

23
00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:40.980
"Where Rostam stood before, I stand today! Haydar! Haydar!"

24
00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:46.380
[Classroom roll call before the strike]
"Amir-Ali Hassanzadeh?" — "Present!"
"Makan Nasiri Fouzani?" — "Present!"

25
00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:53.220
"Amir-Mohammad Ghasemi?" — "Present!"
"Mohammad-Taha Jafari?" — "Present!"

26
00:07:53.480 --> 00:08:10.240
[Interviewer] Can you tell us about the atmosphere at Shajareh Tayyebeh Primary School?
Imagine it is the morning of the incident, an hour before the strike, as girls and boys arrive with their families.

27
00:08:10.500 --> 00:08:20.500
[Surviving Teacher 1] Let me explain that our building housed two schools:
the ground floor was the boys' school, and the second floor was the girls' school.

28
00:08:20.500 --> 00:08:28.120
On Saturdays and Mondays we held morning prayer gatherings.
I was so close with my students that they called me "Mother."

29
00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:38.120
Out of 17 teachers in one shift, only 3 survived — all the others were martyred;
and out of our 19 teachers, only 5 of us survived and 14 were martyred.

30
00:08:38.120 --> 00:08:50.220
[Surviving Teacher 2] Around 10:30 AM, our principal learned that explosions had occurred in Tehran.
Anxious and worried for the children's safety, she came to us immediately.

31
00:08:50.220 --> 00:09:04.820
She told us: "Call the parents right away to come pick up their children."
Immediately, Ms. Nademi, my martyred colleagues, and I called every family to come get the children.

32
00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:18.920
Suddenly a deafening explosion hit, smoke filled the air, and the windows shattered violently.
At that moment, five of my students were standing in the middle of the classroom waiting for their parents.

33
00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:36.920
I heard them scream: "Miss, I'm scared!" I saw fire rushing toward me,
toward Reyhaneh and her sister Mastaneh, and toward Tarannom Rostami.

34
00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:46.000
I pulled them all into my arms — and after that, I heard no one's voice anymore.
Everything was black smoke; we could no longer breathe.

35
00:09:51.960 --> 00:10:07.020
[Bereaved Father 1] At 10:30 AM I was sitting at home when a blast shook the city,
followed by a second explosion. Someone called and said: "They hit Shajareh School!"

36
00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:19.020
I lost all sense of myself and ran the entire way from my house to the school on foot.

37
00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:29.020
[Bereaved Father 2] The teacher had called my wife to come pick up the students;
just as my wife reached the school gate, the missile struck.

38
00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:44.440
[Bereaved Mother] My child had a phone and called me twice: "Mommy, where are you?
Come pick me up, the teachers are having a meeting downstairs." Then Ms. Alaei called to come get the children.

39
00:10:54.200 --> 00:11:08.820
When I reached the school, the building was completely demolished.
No matter how much I searched and screamed "Reyhan! Mahdi!" — nothing was left.

40
00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:22.900
[Father of First-Grader Ali] I climbed onto the rubble, searching for where Ali's classroom had been.
This section where the building completely collapsed was Ali's first-grade classroom.

41
00:11:22.900 --> 00:11:34.100
I stood on the ruins calling out: "Ali, Daddy, where are you? Ali, my son, where are you?"
All through that night, no matter how hard I searched, I could not find him.

42
00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:50.420
[Father of Fourth-Grader] And right here was the fourth-grade classroom —
leveled to the ground with nothing left of it, right next to the school buffet.

43
00:11:50.420 --> 00:12:04.800
Next to the buffet was the fourth-grade classroom, and over there beside the prayer room,
the entire wall was crushed inward.

44
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:25.000
[Surviving Student Melika] We were among the very first students walking out of the school building.
Many children were still inside when the strike hit, and I haven't heard from my classmates since.

45
00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:41.040
[Interviewer] What is your name, and who helped you get out?
[Melika] My name is Melika. Kiana pulled me out.

46
00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:57.900
[Interviewer] Were your classmates inside this classroom?
[Melika] Yes, in the fourth and fifth rows... most of them were martyred... Masoumeh Salehi...

47
00:12:57.900 --> 00:13:22.780
[Melika, weeping] And Liana Mohammadi, who went toward her father — her father was a teacher
in the boys' school, and she was martyred on the way... I loved all of them so much.

48
00:13:22.780 --> 00:13:42.400
[Bereaved Father] When the next explosion struck, the crowd scattered in panic.

49
00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:56.480
Everyone told us to go to the hospital. At the hospital,
every half hour they came out and read the list of the wounded and the martyrs.

50
00:14:16.000 --> 00:14:37.100
[Musical Elegy]
"My darling, living without you has no meaning; all of life's joy is here.
I searched through every word and saw: Homeland is the most beautiful word in this world."

51
00:14:37.100 --> 00:15:03.060
"Even if the sky darkened and left our world, even if the enemy wore the disguise of a friend,
stay beside us until the end of time — the sapling of our patience will bear fruit."

52
00:15:11.560 --> 00:15:24.220
[Red Crescent First Responder] It was approximately 10:47 AM when three terrifying blasts echoed across the sky.
I was in our office and felt as though our own building had been struck.

53
00:15:24.220 --> 00:15:32.580
[Alireza Zarei] How far is your office from here?
[Red Crescent Responder] About 500 meters — our Red Crescent headquarters is just 500 meters away.

54
00:15:32.580 --> 00:15:52.520
I ran down from the second floor as the third missile struck and saw smoke rising from this direction.
We rushed out immediately — we never imagined anyone would bomb an elementary school.

55
00:15:52.520 --> 00:16:12.540
We arrived by ambulance as people cleared the road for us. Entering the schoolyard through smoke and fire,
I cried out: "O Lady Zahra, what has happened here!" Rescue teams, firefighters, Basij volunteers, and locals all rushed in.

56
00:16:12.540 --> 00:16:31.360
I ran across the courtyard to this window and began pulling rubble aside.
Bystanders inside handed me a young schoolgirl through this window.

57
00:16:31.860 --> 00:16:47.920
I held the little girl in my arms — she was only eight or nine years old.
Her entire body was covered in blood, and tragically, she had already been martyred.

58
00:16:47.920 --> 00:17:06.900
[Second Rescue Worker] Right here in this section of the rubble, I had a small trowel in my hand;
flesh was pressed against the walls, and we had to gather the remains into body bags. It was a horrifying scene.

59
00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:27.980
[Red Crescent Responder] The crater you see right here is the exact point of impact
where the missile struck the stairwell between the first and second floors, collapsing the building.

60
00:17:27.980 --> 00:17:44.740
I had only ever seen scenes like this on television in Gaza and Palestine when bombed buildings were dug out —
here in Minab, I witnessed it with my own eyes.

61
00:17:44.740 --> 00:18:02.580
[Alireza Zarei] Was this the most severe tragedy you have ever witnessed in your life?
[Red Crescent Responder] Truly, I had never seen anything of this magnitude — losing 150 to 160 innocent schoolchildren in a single instant.

62
00:18:02.580 --> 00:18:14.820
They were neither military forces nor enriching uranium; they had come to school to study,
sent by mothers and fathers with a thousand hopes for their future.

63
00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:36.620
[Second Rescue Worker] This fourth-grade classroom was the site of the most devastating slaughter of the children.
We found no intact bodies here — the blast wave had crushed them against the walls.

64
00:18:36.620 --> 00:18:55.100
These were small children with fragile bones; we gathered their remains piece by piece into bags,
knowing it would be nearly impossible to identify many of them.

65
00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:12.000
[Ms. Alikouhi, Former Teacher at the School] I can barely speak... They asked me to come identify my colleague.
When I reached her, she was still breathing; I tried to save her, but tragically I could not.

66
00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:25.100
Ms. Taheri, the school principal, was my friend — along with Ms. Askari, Ms. Kamali, and Ms. Mokhtari.

67
00:19:25.100 --> 00:19:36.840
I used to teach at this school myself; upstairs on the balcony and in the prayer room,
I taught the children earthquake safety drills and where to take shelter.

68
00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:46.460
Then they called me: "Ms. Alikouhi, come identify another teacher."
When I stood over her, she had no head on her body — she could only be identified by the ring on her hand.

69
00:19:46.460 --> 00:20:03.900
The children were burned beyond recognition — without hands, without legs.
In this spot we only found their bones and hair.

70
00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:20.500
Fragments of the missile that struck lay right here, reeking of blood,
mixed with burned plastic and children's hair.

71
00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:38.500
[Alireza Zarei] You mentioned finding a teacher's coat?
[Ms. Alikouhi] Yes — the force of the explosion was so immense that the teacher's coat

72
00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:55.500
was blown over the rubble and across the schoolyard wall into the trees —
200 to 300 meters away behind that wall.

73
00:20:55.500 --> 00:21:18.000
[Red Crescent Responder] The highest concentration of martyrs was right here in the exit corridor.
The students tried to flee through this hallway, but the blast collapsed the exit and trapped them inside.

74
00:21:18.000 --> 00:21:44.000
[Staff of Adjacent Teachers' Cooperative Warehouse] Across from the school is our warehouse of household appliances —
refrigerators, freezers, carpets, and bedding belonging to the Ministry of Education's teachers' cooperative.

75
00:21:44.000 --> 00:22:07.660
At the time of the strike I was outside, while my colleagues were inside the warehouse.
It was stocked to the ceiling with bedding, refrigerators, and carpets — and smoke is still rising from it today.

76
00:22:07.660 --> 00:22:23.280
Here is the exact crater where the missile struck the warehouse: within five seconds of the first strike on the school,
our staff ran outside, and the next missile struck right here where our cars were parked.

77
00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:44.880
Look at what is inside: refrigerators, carpet panels stacked to the ceiling,
microwaves, and household appliances.

78
00:22:45.000 --> 00:23:54.000
[Instrumental lament as volunteers gather children's textbooks and schoolbags from the ruins]

79
00:23:55.000 --> 00:24:10.000
[Mortuary Volunteer 1] When we heard the news of the martyrdom of our beloved, innocent children
and teachers at Shajareh Tayyebeh School, we had no peace at home and could not sit still.

80
00:24:10.000 --> 00:24:27.500
First we went to the school, where heavy machinery was lifting the concrete slabs;
the only thing we could do there was gather the children's scattered belongings from the rubble

81
00:24:27.500 --> 00:24:45.180
and sort them in a corner so that at least a keepsake could reach the mothers and fathers
who were waiting with hope for any trace of their children.

82
00:24:45.180 --> 00:25:06.620
Then they announced that women volunteers were needed at the mortuary to prepare the bodies for burial.
It was the first time in my life I had ever entered a mortuary; as I stepped inside, I called out: "O Lady Zainab!"

83
00:25:06.620 --> 00:25:19.120
I had never seen a mortuary before, and I truly do not know where the courage, strength,
and endurance given to us that night came from.

84
00:25:19.120 --> 00:25:34.740
One child we were washing had a broken arm bandaged with cotton;
placing her into the shroud was heartbreaking because her broken arm kept falling limp as we lifted it.

85
00:25:34.740 --> 00:25:51.220
When they brought another child's body to be washed, they told us she could not be washed with water
because her body was torn into pieces and had to receive dry purification (tayammum); we gently wiped the dust from her face.

86
00:25:51.220 --> 00:26:06.240
[Mortuary Volunteer 2] Some of the martyred children had severe burns and blisters with skin hanging loose;
we poured water over them with extreme care so their burned skin would not tear.

87
00:26:06.240 --> 00:26:30.900
Among the martyrs we washed were brilliant Quranic students who could have raised a whole generation —
children who had memorized the entire Quran or five parts of the Quran.

88
00:26:30.900 --> 00:26:44.900
One body had no head or intact torso — only severed limbs. Yet a mother stood there with extraordinary strength,
repeating only: "May God help the heart of Lady Zainab — how did Lady Zainab's heart endure?"

89
00:26:44.900 --> 00:27:44.900
[Traditional Southern Hormozgani mourning lullaby (Sharveh) over the mass funeral procession]
"Lullaby, my sweet flower, sleep my precious daughter..."

90
00:27:44.900 --> 00:28:38.000
"Lullaby, my darling child — scatter flowers upon my beloved's grave..."

91
00:28:38.000 --> 00:28:48.500
[Chapter Four: The Families]

92
00:28:48.920 --> 00:29:01.320
[Mother Speaking at the Mass Funeral] May our children march beneath the banner of the Awaited Mahdi!
By God, we hold our heads high — the pride of our city, our country, and our entire nation!

93
00:29:01.320 --> 00:29:25.000
Let Israel know, let the occupying regime know, let crazed Trump know:
do not think that because we lost our children we are broken or defeated!

94
00:29:25.000 --> 00:29:46.880
From the blood of the youth martyred defending our homeland, we raised innocent children
from whose blood fresh red tulips will bloom!

95
00:29:46.880 --> 00:30:00.020
[Mother of Martyred Student Atena] Atena was first in every single field; she always said:
"I won't accept second place in anything — I must be first and set an example in everything."

96
00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:20.000
[Sister of Mohammad-Taha Jafari] Whenever we asked him: "Mohammad-Taha, what do you want to be when you grow up?"
he would say: "I'll either be a guardian or a pilot — a guardian on the path of Imam Mahdi!"

97
00:30:20.520 --> 00:30:32.680
[Mother of Fatemeh] My wish was for my child Fatemeh to become a doctor one day,
to make her country proud and serve her people.

98
00:30:32.680 --> 00:30:56.500
[Mourning Relative] When they bombed the building, everyone collapsed under the rubble together —
students, parents, and teachers all went to their martyrdom side by side.

99
00:30:56.880 --> 00:31:12.500
[Father of Mohammad-Taha Jafari] Last year I bought him a new four-wheeler and told him:
"Taha, my son, take care of your four-wheeler and don't let the other kids break it."

100
00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:27.740
By God, when I came back, I saw all the neighborhood children riding it around the street.
He told me: "Dad, the other kids don't have one — let them ride it too." He was so kind-hearted.

101
00:31:27.740 --> 00:31:41.960
[Local Shopkeeper] Whenever Taha came to the neighborhood shop,
whatever treat he bought for himself, he bought for the other children too.

102
00:31:41.960 --> 00:31:51.660
[Colleague of Principal Ms. Taheri] Ms. Taheri used to say: "This is my final year; next year I may move to Bandar Abbas,
so I will make this school a renowned brand before I leave." Ms. Taheri made this school known to the entire world and departed.

103
00:31:51.660 --> 00:32:23.160
[Mother of Fatemeh] I made a covenant with God and said: "Fatemeh, my child, after your passing I will build a school
so that for as long as the world endures, students will study in memory of you and our martyred teachers."

104
00:32:37.660 --> 00:33:25.520
[Choral Elegy — "From the Blood of the Homeland's Youth, Tulips Have Bloomed"]
"From the blood of the homeland's youth, red tulips have sprung;
in mourning for their tall stature, the cypress trees have bowed down..."

105
00:33:27.040 --> 00:34:25.480
"How cruel are your turns, O wheel of fate; how unjust are your deeds..."

106
00:34:26.900 --> 00:34:44.900
[Elderly Grandmother mourning in Minabi dialect]
"Let me bring flowers and rosewater for you... May I be sacrificed for your stature, my nightingale..."

107
00:34:44.900 --> 00:35:08.900
[Father of Mohammad-Taha Jafari, weeping] I drove at full speed to the school. When I arrived,
the building had completely collapsed. Moments later, another missile struck beside the school, knocking me to the ground.

108
00:35:08.900 --> 00:35:31.900
Under the rubble, with one of my friends, we found a teacher holding five students in her arms —
God is my witness, five schoolchildren had been martyred right inside their teacher's embrace.

109
00:35:31.900 --> 00:35:45.000
Nothing intact remained of my Mohammad-Taha — only a bloodstained piece of his backpack.
I opened his schoolbag and smelled every single one of his books...

110
00:35:45.500 --> 00:36:44.500
[Mournful woodwind and piano score over images of bloodstained textbooks and schoolbags]

111
00:36:45.000 --> 00:37:00.000
[TV News Anchor] Tonight under the microscope: the strike on the primary school in Minab,
an atrocity surrounded by international scrutiny and undeniable forensic evidence.

112
00:37:00.200 --> 00:37:18.000
[Journalist, in English] "Tell us what you know now about the reported strike on a girls' school in southern Iran on Saturday."
[US Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth] "All I can say is that we're investigating that. We of course never target civilian targets, but we're taking a look and investigating that."

113
00:37:18.200 --> 00:37:30.000
[White House Press Briefing] White House Press Secretary reaction to the strike on the girls' school in Minab:
claiming the United States does not target civilians while an investigation is underway.

114
00:37:30.200 --> 00:37:43.000
[Journalist on Air Force One, in English] "Did the United States bomb a girls' elementary school in southern Iran on the first day of the war and kill 175 people?"
[President Donald Trump] "No, in my opinion, based on what I've seen, that was done by Iran."

115
00:37:43.200 --> 00:38:01.000
[Journalist] "Is that true, Mr. Hegseth?"
[Pete Hegseth] "We're certainly investigating, but the only side that targets civilians is Iran."
[Donald Trump] "We think it was done by Iran. Because they are very inaccurate, as you know, with their munitions. They have no accuracy whatsoever. It was done by Iran."

116
00:38:01.500 --> 00:38:18.440
[Alireza Zarei, standing inside the bomb crater] Look at the scale of this crater!
A missile with this destructive power and blast radius struck a civilian school filled with children.

117
00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:36.000
This vast devastation was caused by American Tomahawk cruise missiles.
They tried to deny it, yet the missile remnants with all their serial numbers and markings are right here, refuting Trump's deliberate fabrications.

118
00:38:41.440 --> 00:39:03.560
[BBC Investigative Report] "The strike on the girls' school in Minab was carried out with a US Tomahawk missile.
Iranian officials report that at least 168 people were killed on the first day of the war, most of them primary schoolgirls."

119
00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:30.280
"New verified footage resolves key questions: in this video you see the moments just before impact and the missile diving into the target.
We showed the footage to weapons experts, who confirmed it is a United States Tomahawk cruise missile."

120
00:39:30.500 --> 00:39:52.000
[Alireza Zarei, inspecting recovered missile fragments] Even Western media outlets — Reuters, The Wall Street Journal, CNN, The New York Times, Middle East Eye, and The Guardian —
confirmed that the munition that struck this school in Minab was an American missile.

121
00:39:52.200 --> 00:40:20.000
These are the actual fragments of the missile that struck Shajareh Tayyebeh School on February 28, 2026:
stamped "BALL AEROSPACE & TECHNOLOGIES CORP — SDL ANTENNA — CAGE CODE 17810 — CONTRACT NUMBER 4200904482 U.S. — MADE IN USA."

122
00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:44.500
[Archival montage of retaliatory missile strikes against US and Israeli military targets]

123
00:40:44.860 --> 00:41:02.940
[Archival Speech] "The US regime entered into direct war because it saw that otherwise
the Zionist regime would face total collapse — yet it achieved nothing and suffered a heavy blow."

124
00:41:03.020 --> 00:41:20.000
[Donald Trump, archival clip] "Israel got hit very hard, especially the last couple of days."

125
00:41:20.500 --> 00:41:56.000
[Echoes of the martyred children's voices in their classrooms]

126
00:41:56.700 --> 00:42:10.500
[Memorial Poem] "You departed, yet you remain forever in our memory; you remain in the crimson memory of the tulips.
O martyrs on the path of love: we are the ones who will pass away, and you are the ones who will endure forever."

127
00:42:10.800 --> 00:42:50.500
[Solo ney flute and piano memorial instrumental through the empty classrooms]

128
00:42:50.860 --> 00:43:11.640
[Alireza Zarei, Closing Summation] In this documentary, we sought to show every dimension of what happened here:
from the bereaved parents of our beloved students, to the teachers who lost their colleagues, the reports of international media,

129
00:43:11.640 --> 00:43:34.740
the volunteers who washed and shrouded over 160 sacred bodies of the martyrs, our Red Crescent responders,
and the citizen who picked up his camera after the first strike and filmed the second impact so this crime would be recorded forever.

130
00:43:34.740 --> 00:43:57.040
The trail of blood across every inch of this soil is etched into history on the foreheads of perpetrators
who commit atrocities against children and deny responsibility even in the face of undeniable proof.

131
00:43:57.040 --> 00:44:25.900
These children departed so that — in the words of their martyred principal who said she would only leave once this school became known to the world —
Shajareh Tayyebeh Boys' and Girls' Primary School in Minab would become known across the entire world.

132
00:44:25.900 --> 00:44:44.000
This school tells the true story of this historic war, and the blood of these innocent children
will seal this chapter in the name of Iran and the dignity of its people.

133
00:44:55.900 --> 00:45:16.520
[End Credits Lament]
"O Mother, my tall palm tree, you left and now my heart is torn at the roots;
day and night my shattered heart burns like wild rue upon the fire..."

134
00:45:16.520 --> 00:45:55.860
"O Mother, the day you came home late from school, my hair turned white —
only God knows my pain, only God knows my pain..."

135
00:45:55.860 --> 00:46:16.760
"May I take your pain upon my soul — cold earth has covered my smile;
until your gentle body was found under the rubble, I died a thousand times..."

136
00:46:18.360 --> 00:46:40.000
"Blessed is your destiny, shining bright above the clouds;
blessed be the milk I gave you, for you were martyred on the path of truth..."

137
00:46:49.760 --> 00:47:24.260
"I grew old from your grief overnight, and now my hands tremble;
the doctor says crying will harm my eyes, not knowing what fire burns inside my chest..."

138
00:47:24.500 --> 00:48:15.000
"My tears fall beyond my control now that my youth is gone;
in the corner of our home I speak with your photograph against my chest, and every Thursday I come to visit your grave, my flower..."
